#### Thinking

TrainInterior is a single mesh 8.04 x 3.7 x 24.17 with Level_Stone material, plus TrainDoor at z=11.94 (end wall door). So the interior is a hollow box mesh, material stone. The door is at the end (front/back), not side. GDD says exit door side? The user wants a side door. In Train, the door is at z=+11.94 (end of car), and the player starts at z=-4. The train door at the end is closed until finale. In Frontier, we need side door; user says "поезд в котором мы едем можно переделать чтобы выход дверь была сбоку". So modify Train car: door on the side, not end.

Current Train interior: 8×24 box, stone material; windows? The mesh may have window holes; only 2 renderers. We need inspect mesh for openings? Could render a screenshot of the train exterior from outside to see. The mesh is likely a room with window openings. In Train, the player sees parallax through windows. The exterior of the same mesh would be a stone box with window holes — that actually can serve as the train exterior! If we duplicate TrainBody into Frontier as the arriving car, it will be exactly the same geometry/scale. But material Level_Stone might look like stone, not metal; still consistent with what the player rode in. The windows exist. The side door? TrainBody may have no side door opening, only end door. User wants side exit. We could cut a side opening? Can't edit mesh easily. But we can hide a section with an added door frame/opening? The mesh is solid; adding a "door" on the side would be a panel on the wall, and the player would walk through the wall (collider mesh) unless the collider is adjusted. Current Frontier train has no collider; the player can't enter the narrow decorative train. For arrival, the player doesn't enter; R13/cart emerge. We can fake a side opening with a dark/black panel placed on the side wall, with cyan frame, and animate characters "through" it — visually they'd clip through the wall, but with a dark opening panel it can read as open. Since the train body is stone with window holes, a black opening with frame looks like a cargo door. The mesh wall remains behind, but the dark panel sits outside and conceals the wall. When R13/cart pass through, they'd emerge from behind the panel; they can clip through the mesh because there is no collider on the train in Frontier. It can look convincing if the opening is opaque and they start behind it.

But user explicitly wants geometry correct and beautiful, "дверь сбоку". A black panel with cyan frame is an illusion; maybe acceptable but not true geometry. Better to build an actual train exterior with a side doorway. Since the interior mesh is one solid box, making a real side opening requires either editing the mesh (Blender) or building a new train exterior mesh. Could build a new train model in Blender matching the interior 8×24×3.2, with side doors/windows, and use it in both scenes? That's a significant asset task but the project has Blender pipeline. The user asks "сделай всё правильно по геометрии".

Alternative: keep the existing TrainInterior mesh in Train, but build a new exterior shell in Frontier with a side opening and windows, matching 8×24×3.7. The exterior doesn't have to be the exact same mesh as interior; it needs to look like the same train: same scale, same window pattern, same colors. Build via Unity primitives (cubes) for body panels around a side opening, windows, roof, undercarriage, headlights. This is doable procedurally in the scene. But "same train" visually requires matching window layout. We can inspect the TrainBody mesh window positions. Maybe simpler: duplicate the TrainBody into Frontier and overlay a side opening; but the mesh has geometry.

Could use the existing TrainInterior mesh as the exterior and create the side opening by "cutting" via a shader? No.

